# XSH 2.9 Threads
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# What XSH 2.9 requires of the system as a whole: thread safety, thread IDs,
# mutex ownership, scheduling attributes, cancellation (states, points,
# cleanup), the atomicity of file operations between threads, application
# managed stacks and process-shared objects in other mappings. Facts a
# function page already tests are not repeated; the .cov file points to the
# assertions that cover them.
#
# The cancellation point tests (012 to 023) share a harness (t/pccp.h):
# each function runs in a thread of its own, with the request made while
# cancelability is still disabled; 024 makes the request while the thread
# is blocked.

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.001
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.1
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.6
class      required
since      2001
says       Four threads released together that each take 200 blocks with malloc()
           and open and dup 25 descriptors each end with blocks that do not
           overlap and still hold their owner's value, and with 200 different
           descriptor numbers.
note       Thread safety of malloc(), open() and dup() (none is on the list of
           functions that need not be thread-safe); handing one descriptor to two
           callers would break the lowest-free-number rule of XSH 2.6 as well.
test       t/001-alloc-concurrent.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.002
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.1
class      required
since      2001
says       strerror_r(), gmtime_r(), rand_r(), strtok_r() and snprintf(), called
           300 times each by four threads at once on data of their own, give
           every thread the results the main thread got before any thread
           started.
test       t/002-reentrant-concurrent.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.003
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.1
anchor     functions/flockfile.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       Four threads calling fputs() 500 times each on one stream with a line
           of 40 identical letters leave a file of 2000 whole lines, each
           uniform, 500 per thread.
note       fputs() is thread-safe (2.9.1) and, like the other stdio calls,
           behaves as if it held the stream's lock for the call, so one call's
           output is not split by another's.
test       t/003-stdio-same-file.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.004
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.2
class      required
since      2001
says       Eight live threads have different IDs, each equal to the one
           pthread_create() stored for it and pthread_self() gives in it, and
           different from the main thread's; the ID of a thread that has
           terminated but not been joined still works with pthread_join(), which
           returns its value.
note       The lifetime of a thread ID ends after the later of termination and
           the end of being joinable, so the unjoined terminated thread's ID is
           still valid.
test       t/004-thread-ids.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.005
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.3
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.6
class      required
says       A pthread_mutex_t, an mtx_t and a write-locked pthread_rwlock_t each
           have one owner at a time: four threads that take the lock 20000 times
           and update a shared counter in two steps never find another thread
           inside and lose no update; readers holding the read-write lock never
           see a writer inside.
note       since is left out: the mtx_t part is from 2024, the others from 2001.
test       t/005-mutual-exclusion.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.006
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.3
class      required
says       A thread owns a pthread_mutex_t after pthread_mutex_lock(),
           pthread_mutex_trylock(), pthread_mutex_timedlock() and
           pthread_mutex_clocklock() succeed and after pthread_cond_timedwait()
           returns (0 or ETIMEDOUT), as shown by another thread's trylock
           returning EBUSY; it does not own it after pthread_mutex_unlock()
           (trylock by another thread returns 0) or while blocked in the
           condition wait (another thread can lock it).
note       Ownership is observed through trylock by a probe thread, whose result
           must be exactly 0 or EBUSY. The timed wait has a 2 s limit and nobody
           signals.
test       t/006-pthread-mutex-owner.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.007
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.3
class      required
since      2024
says       A thread owns an mtx_t after mtx_lock(), mtx_trylock(),
           mtx_timedlock() succeed and after cnd_wait() or cnd_timedwait()
           returns (thrd_success, or thrd_timedout for the timed one), as shown
           by another thread's mtx_trylock() returning thrd_busy; it does not own
           it after mtx_unlock() or while blocked in the wait.
note       As XSH.2.9-threads.006 for the C11 mutex type; the observer is
           mtx_trylock() in a probe thread.
test       t/007-mtx-owner.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.008
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.3
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.6
class      required
says       While one thread is blocked taking a mutex (and, separately, a read-
           write lock for writing) that the main thread holds, a third thread
           that never waits finishes three million loop steps within 30 s.
note       The sentence says a blocked thread shall not prevent an unblocked
           eligible thread from eventually making progress; eligibility depends
           on the scheduling policy, and the 30 s bound is a generous stand-in
           for 'eventually' for a task that needs milliseconds.
test       t/008-blocked-no-starvation.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.009
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.4
class      conditional
when       TSP
since      2001
says       struct sched_param has the members sched_ss_low_priority (int),
           sched_ss_repl_period and sched_ss_init_budget (struct timespec) and
           sched_ss_max_repl (int), which keep the values stored in them.
note       Compile-time and storage check only; the meaning of the values is that
           of the sporadic server policy (XSH 2.8.4).
test       t/009-sporadic-members.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.010
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.4
class      conditional
when       TPS+PS
since      2001
says       An attributes object asking for explicit scheduling with the creator's
           own policy and a priority one above that policy's maximum does not
           give a thread with a priority outside the range of its policy:
           pthread_create() fails, or the thread it creates has a priority in
           the range.
note       The text says the implementation ensures the priority is in range when
           the attributes are used; it does not say by failing. If
           pthread_attr_setschedpolicy() or pthread_attr_setschedparam() already
           refuses the values the test is not in use, since the value never
           reaches thread creation.
test       t/010-priority-range-create.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.011
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.1
class      required
since      2001
says       A thread created by a thread whose cancelability state is DISABLE and
           type ASYNCHRONOUS starts with state ENABLE and type DEFERRED.
note       The text gives ENABLE as the default state and DEFERRED as the type in
           all new threads; XSH.pthread_setcancelstate.001 checks the plain case.
test       t/011-new-thread-state.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.012
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.2
class      required
says       A thread with cancelability enabled (deferred) and a cancellation
           request pending is canceled in open(), openat(), creat(), close(),
           posix_close(), read(), write(), pread(), pwrite(), readv(), writev()
           and fcntl() with F_SETLKW: the call does not return and the cleanup
           handler runs.
note       The assertion rests on the sentence that a call to a cancellation
           point acts on a request already made before it returns. The functions
           are called with arguments that let them finish at once, so a function
           that returns has not acted on the request; side effects of the call
           are not checked (they are those of a call interrupted by a signal). A
           function that blocks despite the request is caught by a 5 s wait and
           counts as a failure, and the names of all failing functions are
           reported.
timeout    300
test       t/012-cp-descriptors.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.013
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.2
class      required
says       As XSH.2.9-threads.012, for accept(), accept4(), connect(), recv(),
           recvfrom(), recvmsg(), send(), sendto() and sendmsg().
note       The assertion rests on the sentence that a call to a cancellation
           point acts on a request already made before it returns. The functions
           are called with arguments that let them finish at once, so a function
           that returns has not acted on the request; side effects of the call
           are not checked (they are those of a call interrupted by a signal). A
           function that blocks despite the request is caught by a 5 s wait and
           counts as a failure, and the names of all failing functions are
           reported.
timeout    300
test       t/013-cp-sockets.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.014
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.2
class      required
says       As XSH.2.9-threads.012, for nanosleep(), clock_nanosleep(), sleep(),
           thrd_sleep(), pause(), poll(), ppoll(), select(), pselect(),
           sigsuspend(), sigtimedwait(), sigwait(), sigwaitinfo(), wait(),
           waitpid() and waitid().
note       The assertion rests on the sentence that a call to a cancellation
           point acts on a request already made before it returns. The functions
           are called with arguments that let them finish at once, so a function
           that returns has not acted on the request; side effects of the call
           are not checked (they are those of a call interrupted by a signal). A
           function that blocks despite the request is caught by a 5 s wait and
           counts as a failure, and the names of all failing functions are
           reported.
timeout    300
test       t/014-cp-wait-sleep.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.015
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.2
class      required
says       As XSH.2.9-threads.012, for pthread_cond_wait(),
           pthread_cond_timedwait(), pthread_cond_clockwait(), cnd_wait(),
           cnd_timedwait(), pthread_join() and thrd_join().
note       The assertion rests on the sentence that a call to a cancellation
           point acts on a request already made before it returns. The functions
           are called with arguments that let them finish at once, so a function
           that returns has not acted on the request; side effects of the call
           are not checked (they are those of a call interrupted by a signal). A
           function that blocks despite the request is caught by a 5 s wait and
           counts as a failure, and the names of all failing functions are
           reported. Each condition wait is made with its mutex locked by the
           calling thread.
timeout    300
test       t/015-cp-sync.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.016
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.2
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       As XSH.2.9-threads.012, for tcdrain() on the slave side of a pseudo-
           terminal with output written to it.
note       The assertion rests on the sentence that a call to a cancellation
           point acts on a request already made before it returns. The functions
           are called with arguments that let them finish at once, so a function
           that returns has not acted on the request; side effects of the call
           are not checked (they are those of a call interrupted by a signal). A
           function that blocks despite the request is caught by a 5 s wait and
           counts as a failure, and the names of all failing functions are
           reported. The pseudo-terminal functions need XSI; the test is not in
           use when no pseudo-terminal can be made.
test       t/016-cp-tcdrain.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.017
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.2
class      conditional
when       FSC
since      2001
says       As XSH.2.9-threads.012, for fsync().
note       The assertion rests on the sentence that a call to a cancellation
