           counts as a failure, and the names of all failing functions are
           reported.
test       t/023-cp-lockf.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.024
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.2
class      required
says       A thread blocked in read() of an empty pipe, recv(), accept(), poll(),
           ppoll(), select(), pselect(), sigwait(), sigsuspend(), pause(),
           nanosleep(), clock_nanosleep(), sleep(), wait(), waitpid(), waitid(),
           pthread_join(), write() to a full pipe, pthread_cond_wait() or
           cnd_wait() is awakened by a cancellation request made 150 ms later and
           is canceled there: the call does not return.
note       Each blocked call has no end or a limit 30 s ahead. The wait for the
           effect is 5 s per function, and the names of all failing functions are
           reported. The text leaves open only what happens if the awaited event
           occurs first, which the test avoids.
timeout    300
test       t/024-cp-blocked.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.025
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.2
class      required
says       About fifty functions that are in neither of the two lists of
           cancellation points (getpid(), dup(), pipe(), kill(), malloc(),
           sigaction(), pthread_mutex_lock(), sem_post(), socket(),
           getsockname(), ftruncate(), rmdir() and so on) return normally in a
           thread with cancelability enabled and a request pending; the request
           is then acted on by pthread_testcancel().
note       Implementations shall not add cancellation points to any other
           function. The functions were checked against both lists, and none can
           block. Whether a request stays pending through all calls is shown by
           the final pthread_testcancel() acting on it.
test       t/025-no-other-cp.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.026
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.3
class      required
since      2001
says       When a thread calls pthread_exit() (having set the asynchronous type)
           and when a thread is canceled in read(), the cleanup handler and the
           key's destructor each find the cancelability state DISABLE and the
           type DEFERRED (pthread_setcancelstate(DISABLE) and
           pthread_setcanceltype(DEFERRED) report them as previous values).
note       The cleanup handler and destructor are the ones the standard says run
           with cancellation disabled; XSH.pthread_cancel.005 covers the handler
           after a cancellation only, and the destructor and pthread_exit() cases
           are added here. The test never enables cancelability in a handler
           (undefined).
test       t/026-cleanup-state.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.027
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.3
class      required
since      2001
says       PTHREAD_CANCELED has type void *, can initialize an object with static
           storage duration, is not null and differs from the address of a global
           object, a static and an automatic object, a heap block and stdout; a
           thread canceled in read() is joined with exactly that value, and a
           thread that returned a null pointer is not.
test       t/027-canceled-constant.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.028
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.2
class      required
since      2001
says       When a cancellation request ends a blocked write() of 8 MiB to a pipe,
           the bytes that reached the pipe are the beginning of the data written,
           they are all there before the cleanup handler runs (the handler reads
           them out; nothing more arrives afterwards), and the thread ends with
           PTHREAD_CANCELED.
note       The text says the side effects of acting on a request in a suspended
           call are those of the call interrupted by a signal (a partial transfer
           of the beginning of the data), and that they occur before the cleanup
           handlers. Not in use if the pipe took all 8 MiB or if nothing had been
           transferred.
test       t/028-cancel-write-prefix.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.029
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.7
class      required
since      2001
says       Four threads that each write 1500 blocks of 4096 bytes (one letter per
           thread) with write() to one descriptor of a regular file leave a file
           of 6000 whole blocks, each uniform, 1500 per letter.
note       write() calls on one descriptor are atomic with respect to each other,
           so neither the data of two calls nor the file offset updates can
           interleave.
test       t/029-fileop-write-write.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.030
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.7
class      required
since      2001
says       Four threads that read a file of 60000 four-byte records to its end
           with read() on one descriptor read every record exactly once and
           whole.
note       read() calls on one descriptor are atomic with respect to each other,
           including the shared file offset.
test       t/030-fileop-read-read.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.031
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.7
class      required
since      2001
says       While one thread repeatedly writes 16384 'A' and 16384 'B' at offset 0
           with pwrite(), two threads reading the block with pread() always get a
           uniform block of 16384 bytes.
test       t/031-fileop-pwrite-pread.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.032
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.7
class      required
since      2001
says       While one thread truncates a file alternately to 0 and 1 MiB with
           ftruncate() and another sets a second file's access and modification
           times to the same whole second with futimens(), fstat() in other
           threads always sees a size of 0 or 1 MiB and equal access and
           modification times.
test       t/032-fileop-trunc-times.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.033
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.7
class      required
since      2001
says       Against a thread that makes a new file and rename()s it over a name,
           stat() and open() of the name always succeed and see a one byte file;
           against link() and unlink() of a second name, stat() sees a link count
           of 1 or 2; against symlink() and unlink() of a link name, readlink()
           and lstat() see either no such file or a whole link to one of two 200
           character targets.
timeout    120
test       t/033-fileop-hierarchy.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.034
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.8
class      conditional
when       TSA+TSS
since      2001
says       A notification function run for a SIGEV_THREAD timer whose
           sigev_notify_attributes holds an application-managed stack runs on
           that stack: the address of one of its automatic variables lies in the
           region.
note       XSH.pthread_attr_getstack.002 covers pthread_create(); this covers the
           sigevent route. The timer is one-shot because the text makes repeated
           generation undefined.
test       t/034-stack-sigevent.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.035
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.9
class      conditional
when       TSH
since      2001
says       A process-shared read-write lock, spin lock and semaphore in a file
           mapped twice at different addresses, initialized through the first
           mapping, work through the second: a write lock taken through one
           blocks tryrdlock through the other, unlock through either releases, a
           spin lock held through one makes trylock through the other fail with
           EBUSY, and sem_post through one is taken by sem_trywait through the
           other.
note       XSH.pthread_mutex_init.005 does the same for the mutex.
test       t/035-altmap-rwlock-spin-sem.c

assertion  XSH.2.9-threads.036
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.9
class      conditional
when       TSH
since      2001
says       A process-shared condition variable and barrier (and their mutex) in a
           file mapped twice, initialized through the first mapping, work through
           the second: a signal sent through the second mapping wakes a thread
           waiting through the first, and a barrier for two released by one
           thread waiting through the first mapping and the main thread arriving
           through the second, with exactly one of them getting
           PTHREAD_BARRIER_SERIAL_THREAD.
test       t/036-altmap-cond-barrier.c
